  note = {Three chapters are cited. Chapter IV, “Habit”: habit as “the enormous fly-wheel of society,” and the warning that the young will “become mere walking bundles of habits.” Chapter IX, “The Stream of Thought”: the “transitive parts” of the stream are felt as truly as its “substantive parts,” so that “we ought to say a feeling of and, a feeling of if, a feeling of but, and a feeling of by, quite as readily as we say a feeling of blue or a feeling of cold” (vol. 1, pages 245 to 246). Chapter X, “The Consciousness of Self”: the present thought appropriates the past ones, and personal identity rests on its finding in them “a warmth and intimacy” it recognizes as its own. The line “we are what we repeatedly do” is Will Durant's compression of Aristotle, not James's and not Aristotle's own sentence.}
